Add a configurable minimum interval between web geolocation queries.

geoclue has no rate limit of any kind. Every source that talks to a web
service queries whenever it is asked to, and the WiFi source is asked to on
every wpa_supplicant scan: on_scan_wait_done() refreshes 1 second after the
BSS list changes, and in any dense area the BSS list always changed. The
result is that the query rate is set by whoever else is scanning, usually
NetworkManager, not by geoclue.

Measured on an idle laptop with one CITY-accuracy consumer (localtimed) and
26 visible APs: one query every 32.8 seconds, sustained, or about 2,600 per
day. That is 8x the 10,000 requests/month free allowance of the Google
Geolocation provider, which the free allowance survives for 3.8 days.
gclue-wifi.c already limits its own rescan to WIFI_SCAN_TIMEOUT_LOW_ACCURACY
(300s) at that accuracy level, but that timer only governs scans geoclue
starts itself, so it has no effect on the observed rate.

There is no way to bound this from the outside. A consumer can only stop
holding a client, which starves it of location without bounding anything else
on the system, and any second consumer reintroduces the full rate.

This adds a global "network/min-query-interval" key, defaulting to 600
seconds, enforced in gclue_web_source_real_refresh_async(), the single point
through which both the WiFi source (by chaining up) and the IP source send
HTTP. Zero or a negative value restores the previous unthrottled behaviour.

Three details worth stating:

- The interval state is file-static, not per-instance. The sources are
  refcounted singletons that are destroyed once the last client drops
  (on_wifi_destroyed clears the static slot), so per-instance state would
  reset on every consumer stop/start and a bursty consumer would evade the
  limit entirely. One budget covers every web source, which is the point: the
  provider bills the machine, not the source.

- A throttled refresh replays the last response rather than returning
  nothing. Consumers have no concept of throttling and must not need one, so
  the location is duplicated with a fresh timestamp and set on the source,
  exactly as gclue_wifi_refresh_async() already does on a cache hit. The task
  itself returns G_IO_ERROR_PENDING, which query_callback() already logs at
  debug rather than warning level, and which makes wifi's refresh_cb() skip
  add_cached_location(). Returning the replayed location instead would bind a
  stale fix to the current BSS key for up to 48 hours.

- The interval is reset in on_network_changed(). Joining a different network
  is the one moment the cached answer is most likely wrong, so it is worth a
  query immediately; without this a machine that travelled while suspended
  would answer with the location of the place it left.

Throttled calls are coalesced rather than dropped: the first one inside a
window schedules a single deferred refresh for when the interval expires, so
a real location change is delayed by at most the interval instead of being
lost until some later scan happens to fire.

An interval of N seconds allows at most 2592000/N queries per 30 days: 4,320
at the 600s default, against the 80,100 measured without it.

